      "answer": "The customer's issue that started after the 2026-02-20 taxonomy rollout is BlueHarbor Logistics. They reported search relevance degradation and increased manual triage time because saved searches returned poor and duplicate results following the taxonomy change on 2026-02-20 [art_8b0063fbb3cb, art_0bccc580184e, art_bd3560dfe194].\n\nThe proof plan proposed to BlueHarbor Logistics to get them comfortable with renewal includes:\n\n- A short-term fix updating index weighting and adding a mapping layer to translate new taxonomy terms to old query terms for the top 20 saved searches.\n- An A/B test in staging starting 2026-03-22 lasting 7-10 business days to validate improvement.\n- Acceptance criteria of top-5 correct hit rate \u2265 80% for prioritized queries.\n- A 1-day production rollout if validation passes.\n- A 90-day professional services engagement at $48k fixed price, with a 30% discount if Orchestrator seat expansion is signed concurrently.\n- A conditional credit clause offering a one-quarter invoice credit if the proof does not meet the agreed improvement within 10 business days.\n- Compatibility testing to ensure no regression in EN-RULES-ENGINE suppression logic and incident correlation latency under 200ms.\n- A fallback reindexing plan if the mapping is insufficient, estimated at 48 hours compute and $25k cost.\n- BlueHarbor agreed to provide schema registry export and anonymized query logs by 2026-03-19 to start the proof.\n- Northstar committed to deliver the proof and conditional credit clause in a SOW addendum.\n\nThis plan was agreed upon in a renewal negotiation call and detailed in a retro document summarizing the taxonomy rollout impact and proof plan [art_0bccc580184e, art_bd3560dfe194].\n\nNo other customer's issue starting after the 2026-02-20 taxonomy rollout or other proof plans for renewal comfort after taxonomy rollouts are identified in the evidence [art_8b0063fbb3cb, art_0bccc580184e, art_bd3560dfe194].",
