      "answer": "There is a recurring Canada approval-bypass pattern affecting multiple Canadian customers, not just MapleBridge. The customers impacted are City of Verdant Bay, Maple Regional Transit Authority, MapleBay Marketplace, MapleBridge Insurance, MapleFork Franchise Systems, MaplePath Career Institute, and MapleWest Bank.\n\nThe shared failure pattern is that after migration from legacy workflow tooling, the approval workflows for region-specific policies fail due to incorrect rule evaluation precedence. Specifically, global-default policies override or bypass country or province-specific approval rules. This causes approvals to be stuck, bypassed, auto-denied, or routed incorrectly. For example, MapleBridge Insurance experienced bypass due to conflicts between 'canada.v2' overrides and 'global.v3' default rules caused by delayed schema registry synchronization. Other customers reported similar issues such as incorrect policy lookup order prioritizing global defaults over province and city overrides, approvals stuck in queues, auto-denials, ignored region overrides, dropped approvals, and routing to global approvers instead of Canada-specific approvers.\n\nIn plain English, the problem is that after migrating to new workflow systems, the system mistakenly applies general global approval rules before or instead of the more specific Canada or province-level rules. This causes the system to skip or mishandle approvals that should require special regional checks, leading to approvals being blocked, bypassed, or sent to the wrong approvers.\n\nThus, MapleBridge is not a one-off case but part of a broader pattern of approval workflow failures across multiple Canadian accounts caused by rule precedence and schema synchronization issues after migration. [art_cbfb5f92862c, art_6be1b68b59cb, art_39c1434aa40a, art_f4a8c516b934, art_e9c20e0a23e0, art_981952a71434, art_b86a0ca2ce1e, art_54e35e2cb431, art_e697b3abe158]",
